          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Solving the problem of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of systems including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            – is notoriously hard. It does however simplify drastically in very low
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensions"} dimensions
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} or higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            and its variants that defines the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (at least as an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
          %p
            The case of dimension 3 is noteworthy, because in this case the quantum theory can be and has been fairly completely understood and is nevertheless non-trivial. Informally, this is due to the fact that behaviour of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            in 3-dimensions is much simpler than in higher dimensions: there cannot be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+waves"} gravitational waves
            in 3-dimensions, hence no “local excitations”. Accordingly, the theory turns out to have a
            %em finite dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
          %p
            More formally, one finds that in 3-dimensions, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"}> Cartan connection
            ), becomes equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of a very well studied 3-dimensional field theory, namely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,1)
            (for vanishing
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+group"} de Sitter group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+group"} anti de Sitter group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,2)
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(3,1)
            (for non-vanishing cosmological constant). See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+gravity"} de Sitter gravity
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+gravity"} Chern-Simons Gravity
          %p
            This means that one can take the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Iso
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            as the
            %em definition
            of 3d
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            = succeed ")." do
